R("Cohort-level effect sizes for synthetic features were estimated using the rank-biserial correlation",
  "and the resulting two-sided meta p-values were corrected across pairs using the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure.",
  "and the resulting two-sided meta p-values were corrected across pairs using the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure. To assess whether the stable pairs remain informative after adjustment for cohort membership, logistic regression models were fitted to the pooled samples of the 15 two-class cohorts for every stable pair, with log10-transformed relative abundances (pseudocount 10^-6) of the two constituents and cohort as a fixed effect; likelihood-ratio tests compared the additive model with the cohort-only model and the model with a multiplicative interaction term with the additive model, with Benjamini–Hochberg correction across pairs (Supplementary Table 9). Spearman correlations between each stable synthetic feature and its constituents were computed on the same pooled data. The 1D significance of individual features is taken from the Holm-adjusted p-values returned by MDFS in 1D mode for every fold and run. As a sensitivity analysis of the training-set composition, the complete LOCO pipeline was repeated with the three cohorts that are never held out (AtezoTRIBE, COLOBIOME, NSHII) removed from every training set (Supplementary Table 8).",
  "Methods for the new reviewer-requested analyses (R5_reviewer_checks/; Reviewer 1 comment 5, Reviewer 2 comments 2, 4, 5).")
P("To evaluate the generalizability of the synergy-detection framework, MDFS was applied independently to each non-CRC disease cohort",
  f"To evaluate the generalizability of the synergy-detection framework, MDFS was applied independently to each non-CRC disease cohort without a LOCO framework, using the full cohort as input. Cohorts were drawn from curatedMetagenomicData; a cohort was analysed if taxonomic and pathway profiles were available, it contained at least ten samples and both disease and control samples ({N['n_analysed']} of {N['n_noncrc']} non-CRC cohorts; Table 1). For each cohort, taxa and pathway abundances were combined into a single feature matrix and normalised to relative abundance. MDFS was run in both 1D and 2D modes ten times with independent random seeds (12-21), applying the same statistical filters as in the discovery arm: Bonferroni-corrected chi-squared pair-level significance and the synergy filter (total_IG > max(base_IG_1D, contributing_IG_1D)). A (base, contributing) direction was retained if it passed both filters in at least six of the ten runs, and its IG values were averaged over the runs in which it passed. Because MDFS reports a pair in each direction that passes the thresholds, with different base and added IG but the same joint IG, all pair counts refer to unordered pairs, represented by the direction with the larger joint IG. For each pair, the synergy gain percentage was computed as ig_gain_pct = IG_2D_added / base_IG_1D × 100, representing how much additional information the contributing feature provides relative to the base feature's individual IG. Pairs with ig_gain_pct ≥ 50% were retained for downstream analysis. Pairs in which at least one feature had zero 1D IG (base_IG_1D = 0 or contributing_IG_1D = 0) yet exhibited positive 2D IG were designated \"emergent\" synergies - interactions in which one feature carries no detectable marginal signal and becomes informative only in the presence of its partner. To identify conserved synergistic markers, unordered pairs observed in ≥ 2 independent cohorts sharing the same disease label were retained; the orientation reported in each cohort is given in Supplementary Table 4.",
  "Validation-arm methods updated to the code actually run (R4_broader_application/run_mdfs_1d_2d.py, load_cohort_data.py: 10 seeds, >= 6 of 10, unordered-pair rule, inclusion criteria).")
